>
  <h2>When is one needed?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A truss, a screen or speakers must be rigged from a venue's roof structure.</li>
    <li>A temporary stage, tower or grandstand is built on site.</li>
    <li>The venue, the inspection body or the commission asks for supporting evidence.</li>
    <li>The configuration changes from one date to the next on a tour.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What a calculation note contains</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A description of the set-up and the assumptions used.</li>
    <li>The loads: equipment weight, suspended loads, dynamic forces.</li>
    <li>The load paths down to the rigging points or the ground.</li>
    <li>Ballast, anchoring and, outdoors, wind loading.</li>
    <li>The associated rigging and layout drawings.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who does what</h2>
  <p>The engineering office models the configuration (Braceworks, Vectorworks, MVR files) and produces the drawings. The <strong>structural engineer</strong> checks and signs the note. The <strong>inspection body</strong> or approved organisation carries out the inspection, depending on the case. The venue provides the capacities of its roof structure or floor.</p>

  <h2>What ESE does</h2>
  <p>Our engineering office produces layout drawings, rigging plots, schematics and studies. Structural calculation notes are checked and signed by a partner structural engineer. We act as the interface with the venue, the inspection body and the suppliers, on a single project as well as on a stadium tour.</p>
</GuidePage>
